## 4. Sensitivity of the decision to economics

The value per visit is held at $1 while the cost per treated user varies:

| Break-even uplift | Chosen depth | Test profit / 1,000 | Random at same depth / 1,000 | Treat all / 1,000 | Model − treat all |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for s in r.sensitivity %}
| {{ s.breakeven_uplift | pp(2) }} | {{ s.chosen_depth | pct(0) }} | {{ (s.test_profit_per_user * 1000) | money }} | {{ (s.test_random_profit_per_user * 1000) | money }} | {{ (s.test_treat_all_profit_per_user * 1000) | money }} | {{ ((s.test_profit_per_user - s.test_treat_all_profit_per_user) * 1000) | money }} |
{% endfor %}

{% set cheap = r.sensitivity[0] %}
**Reading it:** targeting earns its keep when treatment is expensive. At a {{ cheap.breakeven_uplift | pp(2) }} break-even, the model beats treating everyone by only {{ ((cheap.test_profit_per_user - cheap.test_treat_all_profit_per_user) * 1000) | money }} per 1,000 users (no CI computed for this difference). If ads are that cheap, treating everyone is the simpler policy and gives up little.

## Limitations

- A seeded {{ "{:,}".format(r.n_rows) }}-row sample ({{ (r.n_rows / 13979592) | pct(1) }} of the 13,979,592 rows) is used for speed. The full file would tighten every CI.
- The features are anonymised projections, so the targeting rule can't be explained or checked for proxy discrimination.
- Uplift is modelled on *visits*. A conversion-value business case needs a model trained on conversions (0.3% base rate), which would need far more data per segment.
- The data is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, so these results are for non-commercial use.
